      "Ponca people made the Niobrara valley a homeland of earth-lodge villages and corn after separating from the Omaha in the early 1700s; Lakota, Pawnee, and others hunted the Sandhills and canyon. The river’s name is Ponca for running or wide-flowing water. Twenty-one nations used this biological crossroads where eastern woods, boreal relics, and three prairie types meet in a dune sea. Bison and the Ogallala’s springs, not rainfall agriculture, organized the year. That map was intact when the Army arrived.",
